    "q-109735": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109735",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The vector-valued function $r(x,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [\\frac{1}{2},1].$ Which of the following could be $r?$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14284071/assets/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [\\frac{1}{2},1].$  \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{x},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{x}⟩,\\,x\\in [\\frac{1}{2},1],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-109735-a",
              "type": "math",
              "value": "⟨x,\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{x}^{2}}⟩",
